      "About 22 g of protein per scoop for about 110 calories, per the label",
      "Casein content makes a thicker, creamier cereal milk than pure isolate",
      "Turns any cheap cereal or oats into a 30 g protein bowl"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Sweetened with sucralose and stevia",
      "Contains milk and soy, so not for those allergies",
      "You still need to shake or whisk it into the milk first"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The pick for anyone who likes their existing cereal and simply wants more protein in the bowl.",
    "specs": {
      "Protein / serving": "About 22 g per scoop (label)",
      "Calories": "About 110",
      "Sugar / fiber": "Under 1 g sugar, about 1 g fiber",
      "Cost per 25 g protein": "About $1.40 (brand site, Nov 2026)"
    }
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-of/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Protein Cereal",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-protein-cereal/`,
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best protein cereal?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most people, Premier Protein Cereal is the best protein cereal: per the label it has 20 g of protein in a 1-cup, 180-calorie serving, about 11 g per 100 calories, and it is usually the cheapest per gram. Magic Spoon is the best keto option for taste, and Catalina Crunch is the best plant-based, high-fiber choice. Check the label of the flavor you buy."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Which cereal has the most protein?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Of the cereals we compared, Premier Protein Cereal has the most per serving, at 20 g per 1-cup serving per the label. Magic Spoon has about 13 g per cup, Kashi GO about 11 to 12 g per 1 1/4 cups, Catalina Crunch 11 g per half cup and Three Wishes 8 g per cup. Doubling a half-cup serving of Catalina Crunch gives about 22 g."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Magic Spoon actually healthy?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Magic Spoon is a reasonable choice if you want a low-sugar, high-protein cereal: about 13 g of milk protein and 0 g of sugar per cup in most flavors, per the labels. It is sweetened with allulose and monk fruit, has around 7 g of fat and little fiber, and is expensive. It is a better breakfast than sugary cereal, not a health food."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best keto cereal?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Magic Spoon and Catalina Crunch are the two best keto cereals we compared. Magic Spoon lists about 4 g of net carbs per cup in plain flavors, using allulose. Catalina Crunch has 0 g of sugar and a lot of fiber, so its net carbs per half cup are low. Three Wishes and Kashi GO are not keto, despite low or moderate sugar."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does allulose count as sugar?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not on a US nutrition label. The FDA lets manufacturers exclude allulose from total and added sugars because it is not metabolized like table sugar, though it still counts in total carbohydrate. That is why Magic Spoon can show 0 g of sugar. In larger amounts, allulose can cause bloating or digestive upset in some people, so start with one serving."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is protein cereal better than adding protein powder to regular cereal?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not on cost. Adding a scoop of protein powder to the milk on regular cereal or oats usually gives more protein per dollar than any ready-made protein cereal except perhaps Premier. Protein cereal wins on convenience and texture, and Magic Spoon or Catalina Crunch also cut sugar and carbs. Shake the powder into cold milk first rather than sprinkling it on."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is protein cereal good for weight loss?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It can help, because protein and fiber make a breakfast more filling for its calories. Premier Protein Cereal and Catalina Crunch give the most protein per 100 calories. The catch is portion size: half-cup servings are easy to double without noticing. Measure your bowl, use a higher-protein milk, and treat it as part of an overall calorie plan rather than a fix."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: SITE_NAME, url: SITE_URL },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.brand ?? product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Specialty Protein",
    tags: [
      "best protein cereal",
      "high protein cereal",
      "keto cereal",
      "low sugar cereal"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Buyer's Guide"} badge={"Label-Checked Picks"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Best Protein Cereal</Fragment>
      Five high-protein cereals you can buy on Amazon, compared on protein per bowl and per 100 calories, sugar and allulose, fiber, honest serving sizes and cost per 25 g of protein.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#how-we-ranked">How We Ranked</a>
      <a href="#label-math">Protein Cereal Label Math</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Top Picks</a>
      <a href="#sugar-allulose">Sugar, Allulose and Sweeteners</a>
      <a href="#serving-size">Serving Size Realism</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g of Protein</a>
      <a href="#diy">The DIY Alternative</a>
      <a href="#who-should-buy">Who Should Buy Which</a>
      <a href="#buying-on-amazon">Buying Protein Cereal on Amazon</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          A protein cereal is worth buying only if it beats the obvious alternative: a bowl of ordinary cereal or oats with a scoop of protein powder stirred in or shaken into the milk. Most boxes on the shelf do not. Of the cereals we compared, Premier Protein Cereal gives the most protein per bowl and per dollar, Magic Spoon and Catalina Crunch are the low-sugar, keto-friendly options, and Kashi GO is the high-fiber, grain-based pick. Every figure below comes from the nutrition label at the time of writing and varies by flavor. We did not taste-test these cereals ourselves; where we mention taste or texture, it is buyer feedback.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-ranked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Ranked Protein Cereals
          </h2>

          <p>
            We started with every high-protein cereal we could find that is currently sold on Amazon US, then read the nutrition panel of each one. That list included Magic Spoon, Catalina Crunch, Premier Protein Cereal, Kashi GO and Three Wishes. We also looked for a Quest cereal and a Legendary Foods cereal, because both brands come up in searches for protein snacks, but we could not find a current cereal from either; Quest and Legendary sell protein bars, chips and pastries instead. If a product is not listed here, it is usually because we could not verify that it is sold on Amazon or could not source its label.
          </p>

          <p>
            The first measure is protein per serving, but that number alone is misleading. A cereal can claim 20 g of protein by using a big serving size, and another can look weak because its serving is half a cup. So we also calculated protein per 100 calories, which tells you how much of the bowl's energy is protein. Anything above about 9 g per 100 calories is excellent for a cereal; regular breakfast cereals typically land in the low single digits. We then checked sugar, how the label treats allulose and sugar alcohols, and fiber.
          </p>

          <p>
            Serving size realism is the fourth test. Most people pour more than the label serving, especially with half-cup servings. We note where the realistic bowl is likely to be double the printed serving, because that doubles the calories and the cost too. Finally, we worked out the cost per 25 g of protein, the same unit we use across the site, so a cereal can be compared directly with a protein powder, a bar or a ready-to-drink shake.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is a label-analysis guide. We did not run lab tests or blind tastings, and taste or texture notes come from buyer reviews. Prices were gathered from Amazon-linked listings and major grocery retailers during 2026 and move often, so treat the cost column as a guide to the relative order rather than an exact quote. Check the current price, flavor and box size on the listing before you buy.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">By the Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">20 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Most protein per bowl (Premier)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">11 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Best protein per 100 kcal</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">0 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Sugar in Magic Spoon, Catalina, Three Wishes Unsweetened</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">12 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Most fiber per serving (Kashi GO)</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="label-math" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Protein Cereal Label Math: What the Numbers Mean
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with protein per 100 calories, because it is the number that separates a protein cereal from a regular cereal with good marketing. Premier Protein Cereal gives about 20 g in 180 calories, or roughly 11 g per 100 calories. Catalina Crunch is close behind at about 10 g per 100 calories on the 110-calorie label most listings show. Magic Spoon comes in around 9 g, held back slightly by its fat content. Three Wishes is about 6.7 g, and Kashi GO about 6 g.
          </p>

          <p>
            Those numbers matter because breakfast is where many people fall short on protein. If your target is 25 to 30 g at breakfast, which is a common recommendation for spreading protein across meals, a bowl of Premier with a cup of dairy milk gets you there for well under 300 calories. A bowl of Three Wishes with the same milk lands around 16 g. Our guide to <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein you need</a> covers the daily targets in more detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            The protein source also differs, and it is worth knowing which one you are buying. Magic Spoon uses a milk protein blend of casein and whey, which makes it the only dairy-based pick and the one most similar in amino acid profile to a whey powder. Premier relies mostly on wheat gluten and wheat protein isolate with some pea protein, which is why it can hit 20 g in a flake while staying cheap. Catalina Crunch and Three Wishes use pea protein, with chickpea in the case of Three Wishes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fiber is the other label line that changes how full a bowl keeps you. Kashi GO leads by a wide margin at about 12 g per serving. Catalina Crunch adds roughly 6 to 9 g in a half cup, depending on flavor, mostly from potato, corn and chicory root fiber. Magic Spoon and Premier carry only about 1 g. If your goal is a breakfast that holds you until lunch, protein and fiber together beat protein alone.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cereal</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein / serving</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Calories</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein per 100 kcal</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sugar</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Fiber</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Premier Protein Chocolate Almond</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20 g (1 cup)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">180</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 11 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Catalina Crunch Cinnamon Toast</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">11 g (1/2 cup)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 110</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 10 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 6-9 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Magic Spoon Fruity</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 13 g (1 cup)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 140</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 9 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 1 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Three Wishes Unsweetened</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">8 g (1 cup)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">120</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 6.7 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0 g</td>
