        <div id="sugar-allulose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Sugar, Allulose and Sweeteners: Reading Zero-Sugar Claims
          </h2>

          <p>
            Three of the five cereals here show 0 g of sugar on the label, but they get there in different ways. Three Wishes Unsweetened simply has no sweetener: chickpea, tapioca, pea protein and salt. Catalina Crunch uses stevia and monk fruit, which add sweetness with no sugar and essentially no calories. Magic Spoon uses allulose with monk fruit, and allulose is the one that needs explaining.
          </p>

          <p>
            Allulose is a rare sugar that the FDA allows manufacturers to exclude from total and added sugars on the Nutrition Facts panel, because the body absorbs it but does not metabolize it like table sugar. It still appears inside total carbohydrate. That is why a Magic Spoon flavor can list around 14 g of total carbohydrate, 0 g of sugar and about 4 g of net carbs: the brand subtracts both fiber and roughly 9 g of allulose. For keto purposes, that math is broadly accepted, but it is the brand's calculation, not a regulated label line.
          </p>

          <p>
            Allulose is generally well tolerated in small amounts, but larger servings can cause bloating or loose stools in some people, the same way sugar alcohols can. If you plan to eat two cups of Magic Spoon at a time, you are taking in close to 20 g of allulose, so start with one serving and see how you feel. Our guide to the <a href="/best-protein-bars/">best protein bars</a> covers sugar alcohols and allulose in more depth, since bars rely on them even more heavily than cereals.
          </p>

          <p>
            Premier Protein Cereal and Kashi GO use ordinary sugar. Premier lists about 5 g of total sugar with 4 g added, which is modest for a chocolate cereal. Kashi GO lists 8 to 9 g per serving, which is in line with mainstream sweetened cereals. Neither is a problem for most people, but neither is a keto cereal. If you manage blood sugar, our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-diabetics/">protein powder for diabetics</a> guide covers what to watch, and your doctor or dietitian should guide any specific carbohydrate target.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Zero sugar is not zero carbs.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Check total carbohydrate and the sweetener list, not just the sugar line. Allulose and fiber can be subtracted for net carbs; starch from tapioca or chickpea cannot. Three Wishes has 0 g of sugar but about 20 g of total carbohydrate, so it is low-sugar but not keto.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Marshmallow and s'mores flavors are the exception to the zero-sugar pattern. Magic Spoon's Classic Marshmallow and S'mores flavors list about 3 g of sugar because the marshmallow pieces contain real sugar, and their net carbs rise accordingly. It is still a small amount, but if you are buying Magic Spoon specifically for keto, the plain flavors such as Fruity, Frosted, Cocoa and Peanut Butter keep you closest to the advertised numbers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="serving-size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Serving Size Realism: How Much You Actually Pour
          </h2>

          <p>
            Serving size is where protein cereals quietly differ the most. Premier Protein, Magic Spoon and Three Wishes all use a 1-cup serving, which is roughly the size of an ordinary bowl for most adults. Kashi GO uses 1 1/4 cups, a generous bowl. Catalina Crunch uses a half cup, which is small: about a handful in the bottom of a normal cereal bowl.
          </p>

          <p>
            That half-cup serving is not a trick; Catalina Crunch is dense, crunchy and high in fiber, and many buyers say half a cup with milk is enough. But plenty of people pour a full cup, and the label figures then double to about 22 g of protein, roughly 220 calories and 12 to 18 g of fiber. That is a good breakfast nutritionally, but it also halves the number of bowls in a 9 oz bag, to around three and a half, and doubles the cost per bowl.
          </p>

          <p>
            Magic Spoon's 7 oz boxes have a similar problem from the other direction. Each box holds only about five 1-cup servings, which surprises buyers used to family-size cereal boxes. If two people eat it daily, a four-pack lasts barely over a week. That is why the cost per 25 g of protein, not the price per box, is the number to compare.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical approach is to measure your normal bowl once. Pour the amount you would normally eat into your usual bowl, then tip it into a measuring cup. If it is 1 1/2 cups, multiply every label figure by 1.5 for a 1-cup serving, or by 3 for a half-cup serving. It takes a minute and makes the label numbers honest for your own breakfast.
          </p>

          <p>
            Milk changes the numbers too. A cup of dairy milk adds about 8 g of protein, and ultra-filtered milks add more, while most almond and oat milks add only 1 to 4 g. A bowl of Premier with a cup of dairy milk is around 28 g of protein; the same bowl with almond milk is closer to 21 g. If you use plant milk for a dairy-free bowl, Catalina Crunch with a soy or pea milk is the combination that keeps protein highest.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Cost per 25 g of Protein: Cereal vs Powder vs Oats
