        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing protein per serving across different serving sizes</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Catalina Crunch's 11 g comes in a half cup, while Premier's 20 g comes in a full cup. Comparing those numbers directly is misleading. Convert everything to protein per 100 calories or to your real bowl size, and the rankings change. Measuring your normal pour once with a measuring cup fixes this for good.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming zero sugar means keto</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Three Wishes has 0 g of sugar but about 20 g of total carbohydrate from chickpea and tapioca, so it is low-sugar, not keto. Magic Spoon's 0 g of sugar relies on allulose being excluded from the sugar line. Read total carbohydrate, fiber and the sweetener list before treating a cereal as keto-friendly.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Eating several servings of allulose or fiber-heavy cereal at once</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Allulose and added fibers such as chicory root are well tolerated in small amounts, but large bowls can cause bloating, gas or loose stools in some people. If you are new to Magic Spoon or Catalina Crunch, start with a single labeled serving for a week before doubling up.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying premium prices for protein you could add yourself</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A box of Magic Spoon costs roughly three times as much per gram of protein as a decent protein powder. If you already like a regular cereal or oats, a scoop of powder shaken into the milk gets you more protein for less money. Buy a protein cereal for taste or convenience, not for cheap protein.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the milk</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Milk can add anywhere from 1 g to 13 g of protein to a bowl depending on what you pour. Almond and oat milks add very little, dairy adds about 8 g a cup, and ultra-filtered milk adds more. If your bowl seems low in protein, change the milk before you change the cereal.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

